WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.809
This demonstration shows the Kitchen Spurs analytics assignment running locally.

2
00:00:04.809 --> 00:00:12.933
It connects a React restaurant, reusable browser and server tracking clients, event ingestion, SQLite storage, and an analytics dashboard.

3
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:17.701
This is an edited recording of the working application, with synthetic narration.

4
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.235
Navigating to the menu generates a page view through the browser SDK.

5
00:00:23.235 --> 00:00:27.130
The application calls the tracker after each actual route change.

6
00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.587
Query strings and fragments are excluded.

7
00:00:29.587 --> 00:00:37.077
A random visitor identifier persists in local storage, while a session identifier rotates after thirty minutes of inactivity.

8
00:00:37.077 --> 00:00:42.111
These identifiers represent anonymous browser activity, rather than verified people.

9
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.342
Category filtering and adding items generate explicit custom events.

10
00:00:47.342 --> 00:00:52.645
Here, a pizza and citrus cooler produce a cart total of six hundred and forty rupees.

11
00:00:52.645 --> 00:00:56.637
The SDK batches delivery, normally flushing every three seconds.

12
00:00:56.637 --> 00:01:00.006
Its bounded session storage queue can survive reloads.

13
00:01:00.006 --> 00:01:06.993
Transient failures retry with backoff and the same event identifiers, allowing ingestion to deduplicate replays.

14
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.543
Placing the order calls the business API.

15
00:01:11.543 --> 00:01:15.893
Prices are calculated from the server menu, not trusted from the browser.

16
00:01:15.893 --> 00:01:20.542
The order and its order created event are committed in one SQLite transaction.

17
00:01:20.542 --> 00:01:23.700
If event persistence fails, the order rolls back too.

18
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.136
A repeated request with the same idempotency key returns the existing order instead of creating another one.

19
00:01:30.136 --> 00:01:32.877
No payment is collected in this demonstration.

20
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:38.210
The dashboard reads authenticated API reports from persisted events.

21
00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:44.194
It shows page views, distinct anonymous visitor identifiers, total events, orders, and order value.

22
00:01:44.194 --> 00:01:50.177
The two orders shown include an earlier review order and the new six hundred and forty rupee order.

23
00:01:50.177 --> 00:01:52.353
These values come from the database.

24
00:01:52.353 --> 00:01:56.403
Dashboard navigation itself is excluded from browser page tracking.

25
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.344
Popular pages and the event breakdown reveal how people use the restaurant.

26
00:02:02.344 --> 00:02:07.832
Recent activity shows the new order as a server event alongside browser checkout and cart events.

27
00:02:07.832 --> 00:02:11.001
The same anonymous visitor identifier connects the flow.

28
00:02:11.001 --> 00:02:13.774
The browser cannot submit an order created event.

29
00:02:13.774 --> 00:02:20.054
The ingestion endpoint assigns the source, and trusted server ingestion requires a separate private credential.

30
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.180
The Today preset narrows the report to one UTC day.

31
00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.348
Next, both date pickers select the previous day, which has no events.

32
00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:32.274
All metrics become zero and the activity lists show empty states.

33
00:02:32.274 --> 00:02:40.489
Date boundaries are inclusive calendar dates, implemented in SQL with an inclusive start and an exclusive end at the following midnight.

34
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.826
Restoring the seven day range brings the recorded activity back.

35
00:02:45.826 --> 00:02:51.822
Stable event identifiers make retried persistence idempotent, but browser delivery remains best effort.

36
00:02:51.822 --> 00:02:56.072
Closing a tab, clearing storage, or exhausting the queue can lose events.

37
00:02:56.072 --> 00:02:59.798
The standalone server helper uses bounded authenticated retries.

38
00:02:59.798 --> 00:03:06.260
External business systems should persist an outbox with their own business transaction for crash safe delivery.

39
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:15.435
SQLite keeps setup simple and provides real transactions, but synchronous queries and a single writer limit throughput.

40
00:03:15.435 --> 00:03:22.339
Payload validation, body and batch limits, timestamp checks, origin restrictions, and rate limiting bound abuse.

41
00:03:22.339 --> 00:03:25.421
A public browser key is only a project identifier.

42
00:03:25.421 --> 00:03:33.804
Before production, add proper user authentication, retention controls, monitoring, backups, and distributed ingestion and rate limiting.

43
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:42.471
The public repository includes setup instructions, SDK examples, architecture, assumptions, and known limitations.

44
00:03:42.471 --> 00:03:45.833
With Node twenty four, run npm ci, then npm run dev.

45
00:03:45.833 --> 00:03:52.816
Fourteen automated tests, the production build, formatting checks, and GitHub continuous integration passed.

46
00:03:52.816 --> 00:04:00.898
The tests cover retries, deduplication, order replay, transaction rollback, authentication, date boundaries, and persistence.

47
00:04:00.898 --> 00:04:03.614
Thank you for reviewing the demonstration.
